WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.000
MUSIC

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:07.480
By bringing unseen creatures to television,

00:00:07.480 --> 00:00:11.280
Sir David Attenborough has himself become the rarest of species.

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:14.320
A broadcaster, his programmes reach huge audiences,

00:00:14.320 --> 00:00:19.000
but he's also known for the intelligence of his views on science and broadcasting.

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:47.000
MUSIC

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:51.000
Sir David Attenborough has combined science and entertainment

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:55.000
in a succession of natural history series screened and praised around the world,

00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:59.000
including Life on Earth, The Living Planet and The Life of Birds.

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:04.000
I get to see the most fantastic things.

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:09.000
We've been seeing things in the last couple of weeks

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:12.000
that no human being's seen before.

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:14.000
Can you imagine?

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:19.000
And as a former controller of BBC Two and managing director of BBC Television,

00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:24.000
he's also one of the most respected voices in the debate on the future of TV.

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:27.000
But as David Attenborough ultimately found the jungle

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:30.000
a more comfortable environment than the boardroom,

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:33.000
it seemed fitting to meet at the Natural History Museum.

00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:39.000
You're in your late 70s now, looking back, the life you've had,

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:43.000
how does it measure up to what you might have imagined in your early years?

00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:45.000
In your 20s, 30s?

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:50.000
Well, pretty well, really.

00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:54.000
I couldn't have dreamt that I'd done what I'd done.

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:58.000
Of course, when I was in my teens and thinking what I was going to do,

00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:01.000
I thought I was going to be a scientist.

00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:05.000
And in many ways I regret not being a scientist.

00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:08.000
But I couldn't possibly have imagined that I was going to be in television

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:11.000
because there wasn't any television.

00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:13.000
Not effectively, anyway.

00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:17.000
In 1938, there was just the beginning of television, but I didn't see it.

00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:19.000
So I didn't know.

00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:22.000
And I couldn't have imagined that it would have opened so many doors

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:25.000
and paid so many air tickets.

00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:27.000
You're filming at the moment the next big series,

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:29.000
which will be Life in the Undergrowth.

00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:32.000
At a very simple level, because you don't have to do it financially, I assume,

00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:34.000
why do you still do it?

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:37.000
Oh, that's not a very difficult question.

00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:41.000
Because it's enormously interesting.

00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:44.000
I mean, I get to see the most fantastic things.

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:50.000
We've been seeing things in the last couple of weeks

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:53.000
that no human being's seen before.

00:02:53.000 --> 00:02:55.000
Can you imagine?

00:02:55.000 --> 00:02:59.000
What a fantastic privilege and excitement.

00:02:59.000 --> 00:03:03.000
Such as? Give me an example of something you've seen.

00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:09.000
Well, we've been watching ants called matabele ants,

00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:14.000
which live by raiding termites.

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:18.000
And we've been able to put new electronic gear,

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:23.000
tiny little cameras, inside to see what happens.

00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:29.000
And these matabele ants come along and tackle the huge soldier termites

00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:34.000
and have enormous armed heads, you know, with helmets of chitin.

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:39.000
And they said about them, and nobody's ever seen described how they do it.

00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:44.000
What they actually do is to seize the jaw, one jaw of this termite soldier,

00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:47.000
and then bring round their abdomen, which is armed with a sting,

00:03:47.000 --> 00:03:55.000
and sting them in the one place where you could actually sting a termite soldier anyway.

00:03:55.000 --> 00:03:57.000
And that is in the mouth.

00:03:57.000 --> 00:03:59.000
I mean, it's all for the sea, actually.

00:03:59.000 --> 00:04:03.000
And this juttering termite is hauled away by the rest of the soldiers.

00:04:03.000 --> 00:04:08.000
Nobody's ever watched that kind of thing going on before, in that degree of detail.

00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:16.000
In the Natural History Museum, there's a fossil named after Attenborough.

00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:19.000
This, rather than television series, would have been the ambition

00:04:19.000 --> 00:04:21.000
of a schoolboy interested in science,

00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:26.000
growing up in Leicester as the son of a teacher.

00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:28.000
You are to some extent a performer.

00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:31.000
Your brother, Dickie, better known as Lord Attenborough,

00:04:31.000 --> 00:04:34.000
he is very much a fine actor, a director.

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:38.000
There must somewhere have been a showbiz gene, was there?

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:40.000
Well, I don't know.

00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:45.000
I suppose there has to be some kind of histrionic gene or something.

00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:50.000
I never intended, could have gone into the theatre or feature films.

00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:53.000
I mean, that is a world I know nothing about.

00:04:53.000 --> 00:05:00.000
And I feel still very uneasy in it, I mean, when I'm alongside him.

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:03.000
I mean, it's not my world at all.

00:05:03.000 --> 00:05:09.000
And I dare say, he'd probably say, that his world wasn't the world of the entomologist.

00:05:09.000 --> 00:05:15.000
But I suppose that actually a good teacher has to be something of an actor.

00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:18.000
And I dare say an actor has to be something of a teacher.

00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:24.000
I'm interested in the upbringing because Richard Attenborough's films are very often,

00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:26.000
they're liberal, they're campaigning films.

00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:29.000
I mean, obviously, Gandhi, Bico and so on.

00:05:29.000 --> 00:05:31.000
Your programmes, although they're good programmes,

00:05:31.000 --> 00:05:35.000
they also seek to do a certain amount of good, to educate, to spread messages.

00:05:35.000 --> 00:05:38.000
Is that something you were brought up with, do you think?

00:05:38.000 --> 00:05:45.000
Yes, my parents were certainly people with strong social consciences.

00:05:45.000 --> 00:05:49.000
And we were certainly brought up to feel that we should have a social conscience

00:05:49.000 --> 00:05:54.000
and have some regard for the society of which we were part.

00:05:54.000 --> 00:06:00.000
And, yeah, I mean, we were taught that was a proper thing to do.

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:04.000
And I think during the Second World War your family took in refugees.

00:06:04.000 --> 00:06:07.000
Oh, yes, and before.

00:06:07.000 --> 00:06:12.000
My mother, I have an early memory of my mother herding us up, the three of us,

00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:18.000
her three sons, and taking us to a big hall outside Leicester where we lived

00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:21.000
and said we had to clean this hall because there were Basque children

00:06:21.000 --> 00:06:25.000
who had been bombed during the Spanish Civil War.

00:06:25.000 --> 00:06:28.000
And these children had come and they'd lost their mothers and they'd lost their fathers

00:06:28.000 --> 00:06:31.000
and they were going to live here and we've got to do something for them.

00:06:31.000 --> 00:06:35.000
And so my mother actually got down and scrubbed the floors.

00:06:35.000 --> 00:06:44.000
And then they were involved very much in getting Jewish refugees from Germany.

00:06:44.000 --> 00:06:49.000
And that was because, and they did so through academic areas

00:06:49.000 --> 00:06:55.000
because my father was an academic and so they looked after, as far as they could,

00:06:55.000 --> 00:07:02.000
doctors and physicists and chemists and all kinds of people who came from Germany.

00:07:02.000 --> 00:07:07.000
The question of God arises obviously for anyone who studies the natural world as you have.

00:07:07.000 --> 00:07:08.000
Was it a religious upbringing?

00:07:08.000 --> 00:07:10.000
Not at all, no.

00:07:10.000 --> 00:07:13.000
And have you at any time had any religious faith?

00:07:13.000 --> 00:07:15.000
No.

00:07:15.000 --> 00:07:19.000
And so, I mean, your programmes clearly are Darwinist,

00:07:19.000 --> 00:07:25.000
but it's not something you never seem to actually take on the creationists who, for example, are...

00:07:25.000 --> 00:07:29.000
Oh, I certainly do, privately, and would quite happily do.

00:07:29.000 --> 00:07:33.000
It's not the place to do it in the sort of programmes I make.

00:07:33.000 --> 00:07:36.000
But I don't have...

00:07:36.000 --> 00:07:42.000
I mean, I have a very vigorous correspondence with a number of religious fundamentalists.

00:07:42.000 --> 00:07:49.000
And I have a pretty straightforward answer, really, which is that all societies,

00:07:49.000 --> 00:07:56.000
all societies have had need to find an explanation of the way in which human beings came into the world.

00:07:56.000 --> 00:08:01.000
And the Australian Aboriginals think it's a rainbow serpent in the sky,

00:08:01.000 --> 00:08:06.000
and the Thais think that it's a sea of milk being churned by demons,

00:08:06.000 --> 00:08:13.000
and that a tribe of people in the Middle East thought that it was a garden

00:08:13.000 --> 00:08:19.000
in which the first woman was made by taking a rib from an atom.

00:08:19.000 --> 00:08:21.000
Now, they can't all be right.

00:08:21.000 --> 00:08:24.000
Some of them have to be wrong.

00:08:24.000 --> 00:08:28.000
So which are right? Well, they're only faced with such conflict.

00:08:28.000 --> 00:08:32.000
Why don't we look at the world around us, as it is in this institution,

00:08:32.000 --> 00:08:38.000
and try and make sense of that, because this evidence is the same everywhere.

00:08:38.000 --> 00:08:45.000
And I find it far more awesome, wonderful, that it should have...

00:08:45.000 --> 00:08:49.000
that creation, our appearance in the world, should be the culmination,

00:08:49.000 --> 00:08:56.000
or at least one of the latest products of 3,000 million years of organic evolution,

00:08:56.000 --> 00:09:03.000
than a kind of country trick from taking a rib out of a man's side in a trance.

00:09:03.000 --> 00:09:06.000
But the word wonder is important, because, as you know,

00:09:06.000 --> 00:09:10.000
because you get letters from creationists, I would imagine, particularly who watch Fregeo in America.

00:09:10.000 --> 00:09:15.000
A lot of people take that leap from a sense of wonder in nature to assuming there must be something behind it.

00:09:15.000 --> 00:09:19.000
When you look and you clearly have that sense of wonder, that sense of awe of the natural world,

00:09:19.000 --> 00:09:24.000
you are thinking, what, this just... it just happened. It's just...

00:09:24.000 --> 00:09:28.000
I don't know.

00:09:28.000 --> 00:09:36.000
I can't believe that each species was brought into existence by a merciful God

00:09:36.000 --> 00:09:39.000
who cares about human beings for obvious reasons.

00:09:39.000 --> 00:09:45.000
I mean, for the fact that why is there so much pain, why is there so much disaster,

00:09:45.000 --> 00:09:50.000
why are some animals tortured in so many ways,

00:09:50.000 --> 00:09:53.000
and why are human beings tortured by all kinds of parasites?

00:09:53.000 --> 00:09:58.000
I can't believe that God created parasites in order to torture small children.

00:10:01.000 --> 00:10:07.000
Edinburgh's avowed secularism leaves the question of what drives an almost evangelical passion

00:10:07.000 --> 00:10:10.000
to communicate the facts and wonders of nature.

00:10:14.000 --> 00:10:19.000
If there were a Hollywood movie about you, there would be a moment when you first looked at a fossil

00:10:19.000 --> 00:10:23.000
or the lightbulb moment. Is there such a moment when the realisation came?

00:10:23.000 --> 00:10:30.000
Oh, yeah. I mean, I know the kind. I know it's kind because it repeated itself.

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:35.000
It's that moment when you hit a rock and it falls in two halves

00:10:35.000 --> 00:10:44.000
and there is this astonishing, beautiful, shining, glittering, wonderful, organic shape, a shell,

00:10:44.000 --> 00:10:49.000
or indeed a creature with legs.

00:10:49.000 --> 00:10:54.000
And nobody's ever seen that before except you. You're the first person to do it.

00:10:54.000 --> 00:10:58.000
And that's thrilling. I mean, I find that thrilling still.

00:10:58.000 --> 00:11:01.000
Given what happened to you, it's become like the story of the person who said

00:11:01.000 --> 00:11:07.000
the Beatles would never come to anything, but you were rejected, first of all, in radio you tried.

00:11:07.000 --> 00:11:13.000
Yes, and I saw an advertisement for the BBC. The advertisement was for a radio producer.

00:11:13.000 --> 00:11:17.000
And I thought, well, I'm supposed to be thinking up ideas for books, for science books.

00:11:17.000 --> 00:11:20.000
Why can't I think up ideas for radio?

00:11:20.000 --> 00:11:25.000
And so I applied and didn't get an interview even.

00:11:25.000 --> 00:11:28.000
But I, fortnight later, got a letter from someone saying,

00:11:28.000 --> 00:11:33.000
we've got this new thing, it's only been going for a few years, a couple of years, three years,

00:11:33.000 --> 00:11:36.000
and it's called television.

00:11:36.000 --> 00:11:40.000
And a lot of people are rude about it, but we think, you know, it could be something there.

00:11:40.000 --> 00:11:43.000
Would you like to come and have a go?

00:11:43.000 --> 00:11:46.000
And I took her on the high line, actually.

00:11:46.000 --> 00:11:51.000
I said, well, what security have I got? I was married, I've got a child.

00:11:51.000 --> 00:11:55.000
What do you give me? And they said, we can't guarantee that you'll give you a job

00:11:55.000 --> 00:11:58.000
for more than three months.

00:11:58.000 --> 00:12:02.000
And I thought, well, I can't do that. So I said, certainly not.

00:12:02.000 --> 00:12:06.000
And they said, well, you know, it's only, and I forgot what the figure was, it was a tiny figure,

00:12:06.000 --> 00:12:09.000
but it was actually three times as much as I was earning as publishing.

00:12:09.000 --> 00:12:14.000
I thought, well, give it a go. So I went up to Alexander Palace and just had the time of my life.

00:12:14.000 --> 00:12:16.000
I thought it was absolutely marvellous.

00:12:16.000 --> 00:12:18.000
That's the thing, when you look back at people's careers,

00:12:18.000 --> 00:12:22.000
very often there's this element of luck that something happens that leads them to what they did.

00:12:22.000 --> 00:12:27.000
And that happened to you, because ZooQuest, you should have been a producer and you ended up presenting.

00:12:27.000 --> 00:12:35.000
Yes. I put up an idea that we should cover an expedition on film to West Africa

00:12:35.000 --> 00:12:39.000
that the London Zoo was sending to collect animals for the zoo.

00:12:39.000 --> 00:12:46.000
And the idea was that the man from the zoo, a nice man called Jack Lester, who was curator of reptiles,

00:12:46.000 --> 00:12:52.000
we would film him bouncing on some Caboon Vipo, enormous python or something.

00:12:52.000 --> 00:12:57.000
And then we would show that sequence and then from the film we would come to Jack in the studio

00:12:57.000 --> 00:13:03.000
wrestling with his pythons here while he told us about the intimate details of its anatomy.

00:13:03.000 --> 00:13:10.000
And poor Jack did the first programme, but he was very ill and he really couldn't do the second.

00:13:10.000 --> 00:13:12.000
I mean, he went to Hadcock Hospital.

00:13:12.000 --> 00:13:16.000
And because it had this live element in it, the head of television said,

00:13:16.000 --> 00:13:18.000
well, it's in the Radio Times because someone's got to do it.

00:13:18.000 --> 00:13:20.000
You'd better do it. You're the only bloke there who was there.

00:13:20.000 --> 00:13:23.000
So somebody else took over the cameras and I went and did it.

00:13:23.000 --> 00:13:29.000
But staff no fee, I hasten to add, as the phrase was in those days.

00:13:29.000 --> 00:13:36.000
A month ago, Charles Lagos and I returned from spending four months in search of a dragon.

00:13:36.000 --> 00:13:40.000
And for the next ten years we went on doing that sort of thing.

00:13:40.000 --> 00:13:43.000
But my job as a producer, I was paid as a producer,

00:13:43.000 --> 00:13:47.000
and the fact I appeared in front of the camera was incidental.

00:13:47.000 --> 00:13:50.000
But in theatre when that happens, which it does quite a lot,

00:13:50.000 --> 00:13:53.000
that an understudy or an extra gets to go on and become a star,

00:13:53.000 --> 00:13:57.000
it's what they've been waiting for, they've been dreaming of, but it wasn't in your case.

00:13:57.000 --> 00:14:03.000
No, not at all. But you soon saw that it gave you a weapon.

00:14:03.000 --> 00:14:08.000
I mean, it gave me a chance to say I wanted to do this, that and the other,

00:14:08.000 --> 00:14:15.000
which I might not have been able to do so quite as effectively if I didn't appear in the thing.

00:14:15.000 --> 00:14:18.000
You have good nerves for it though, because something happened,

00:14:18.000 --> 00:14:21.000
I think it was the fourth series of The Equest, you went to New Guinea,

00:14:21.000 --> 00:14:25.000
and something really quite alarming happened and you do seem to have strong nerves.

00:14:25.000 --> 00:14:36.000
No, I don't think so. I know a lot of people are much, as it were, strongly nerve than me.

00:14:36.000 --> 00:14:42.000
I mean, that one you're talking about was just, I couldn't think of what else to do really.

00:14:42.000 --> 00:14:50.000
It was that we met some people at a tribal frontier and we thought we had been ambushed by them

00:14:50.000 --> 00:14:54.000
and they came galloping down the path, waving spears to us.

00:14:54.000 --> 00:15:01.000
I simply couldn't think of what else to do. I mean, the camera was turning and it happened,

00:15:01.000 --> 00:15:07.000
because I had been talking about where we were and suddenly these people burst out from hiding.

00:15:07.000 --> 00:15:11.000
And the only thing I could think of doing, and nobody was running, I mean, you couldn't run,

00:15:11.000 --> 00:15:18.000
so the only thing to do was to go to them and say hello. It's what I did.

00:15:18.000 --> 00:15:23.000
To my enormous relief, they greeted me not fiercely, but with considerable enthusiasm.

00:15:23.000 --> 00:15:30.000
Laughing at myself, I discovered that this, in fact, is merely the normal New Guinea welcome.

00:15:30.000 --> 00:15:32.000
And were you scared?

00:15:32.000 --> 00:15:42.000
Yes, I suppose so. I scare quite easy. I mean, at the time I've been frightened quite a lot really.

00:15:42.000 --> 00:15:47.000
I'm interested, if we talk about the moment that turns up in all those programmes,

00:15:47.000 --> 00:15:52.000
the greatest moments of TV ever in Rwanda when the guerrilla comes up behind you,

00:15:52.000 --> 00:15:55.000
I'm interested in what you were thinking. Were you thinking this is an astonishing thing to happen,

00:15:55.000 --> 00:16:01.000
or were you thinking this is amazing TV? Is that calculation ever there?

00:16:01.000 --> 00:16:10.000
No, I mean, I think that was such an overwhelming experience and caught me so by surprise.

00:16:10.000 --> 00:16:18.000
The television part of it is, well, it's a long way down in the priorities really.

00:16:18.000 --> 00:16:23.000
There I was thinking that this guerrilla was going to be behind me

00:16:23.000 --> 00:16:30.000
and that I was going to be talking and then suddenly these little baby guerrillas

00:16:30.000 --> 00:16:36.000
come and start to take my shoes off and the female put her hand on my head.

00:16:36.000 --> 00:16:40.000
And you don't think, is this going to be a terrific shot?

00:16:40.000 --> 00:16:46.000
And I can tell you, you think, holy mackerel, look at this extraordinary thing,

00:16:46.000 --> 00:16:51.000
and she's treating me in this amazing way.

00:16:51.000 --> 00:16:57.000
There is a temptation dealing with animals, wildlife, nature towards sentimentality,

00:16:57.000 --> 00:17:03.000
which Disney represents obviously. You have resisted that quite deliberately.

00:17:03.000 --> 00:17:08.000
Yes, I think that what interests me about the natural world is the way it works,

00:17:08.000 --> 00:17:13.000
which is, and trying to really understand how it works,

00:17:13.000 --> 00:17:21.000
and that involves understanding about violence, understanding about predators,

00:17:21.000 --> 00:17:31.000
and understanding about insects and the way they affect the way the whole machinery goes,

00:17:31.000 --> 00:17:36.000
which is nothing to do with sort of a pop mummy frog, you know.

00:17:36.000 --> 00:17:41.000
We're at a stage now technologically where anything can be faked essentially visually.

00:17:41.000 --> 00:17:45.000
There have been cases in wildlife films where things have been computer enhanced,

00:17:45.000 --> 00:17:50.000
have been faked, animals who don't exist are shown moving around. Does that worry you?

00:17:50.000 --> 00:18:01.000
Yes, a lot. And I worry that people, the natural world is so astonishing that every now and again,

00:18:01.000 --> 00:18:07.000
I mean, the series I'm doing now, which is about earthworms and scorpions and stuff,

00:18:07.000 --> 00:18:13.000
that people will say, oh, well, that's not really true, you know, they don't do that sort of thing,

00:18:13.000 --> 00:18:18.000
they don't communicate in that sort of way, that's just the way that they are doing it these days.

00:18:18.000 --> 00:18:22.000
They can model anything, they can make dinosaurs come to life, you know,

00:18:22.000 --> 00:18:27.000
and who knows, they don't really do that sort of stuff, and that's terrible.

00:18:27.000 --> 00:18:35.000
And so I think one of the few justifications for having people appear in natural history programs

00:18:35.000 --> 00:18:42.000
in the way that I do is that people may say, well, if he says it's right, it's right,

00:18:42.000 --> 00:18:52.000
and that the presenter can give veracity and persuade people that it is true.

00:18:52.000 --> 00:19:00.000
And that's why it's extremely important that I don't ever move into that area and try and deceive people about things.

00:19:00.000 --> 00:19:03.000
But in one of your own series there was an instance which the journalist Matthew Parris

00:19:03.000 --> 00:19:06.000
wrote about at some length in which a polar bear is seen giving birth,

00:19:06.000 --> 00:19:11.000
and the birth, it turns out, was filmed in a zoo, put together with footage from the Arctic.

00:19:11.000 --> 00:19:13.000
That was justifiable.

00:19:13.000 --> 00:19:21.000
In my view, totally, because what the program was about was trying to tell you about the natural history of polar bears.

00:19:21.000 --> 00:19:27.000
And one of the important things to understand about bears, particularly polar bears,

00:19:27.000 --> 00:19:31.000
is that they give birth during hibernation and they produce this tiny little baby.

00:19:31.000 --> 00:19:33.000
I mean, that's a key thing.

00:19:33.000 --> 00:19:38.000
And so I had no hesitation at all about putting together footage from all over the place,

00:19:38.000 --> 00:19:46.000
including unique material of a female polar bear giving birth, which was shot in Hamburg Zoo.

00:19:46.000 --> 00:19:53.000
Now, if I had said, well, here I am in the Arctic and I'm trying to stalk this polar bear

00:19:53.000 --> 00:19:59.000
and I'm trying to get a look inside her den and then put that in, then that's a lie.

00:19:59.000 --> 00:20:03.000
I mean, that is totally unjustifiable.

00:20:03.000 --> 00:20:06.000
But if you're saying, I'm trying to tell you about the things that happen about polar bears,

00:20:06.000 --> 00:20:12.000
how they swim, how they give birth, how they hunt, and you put together stuff from all over, that's fine.

00:20:12.000 --> 00:20:14.000
And we didn't make any secret of it.

00:20:14.000 --> 00:20:21.000
We gave a credit at the end of the program saying thanks were due to the Hamburg Zoo.

00:20:21.000 --> 00:20:29.000
Many people have compared the higher levels of management of the BBC to a jungle.

00:20:29.000 --> 00:20:35.000
Edinburgh is one of the few people to have direct experience of both savage environments.

00:20:45.000 --> 00:20:47.000
Apart from your many, many appearances on screen,

00:20:47.000 --> 00:20:54.000
you had a brief spell as a BBC manager running BBC Two and then running the television service.

00:20:54.000 --> 00:21:03.000
In general, was TV better at that period in the 60s and 70s than it is now?

00:21:03.000 --> 00:21:07.000
Well, it wasn't all that brief. I mean, I was there for eight years.

00:21:07.000 --> 00:21:16.000
And of course, it would be dishonest of me not to say that I thought that we did some good things in that time.

00:21:16.000 --> 00:21:24.000
I mean, I'd be ashamed if after eight years you said, oh, well, it was a failure.

00:21:24.000 --> 00:21:32.000
But we had the opportunity to do things which are much more difficult to do now.

00:21:32.000 --> 00:21:38.000
There are only three networks in the country. The BBC had two of them.

00:21:38.000 --> 00:21:47.000
If the BBC didn't use that position of great scope that it had of two networks to produce something, you know,

00:21:47.000 --> 00:21:55.000
that wasn't pretty good and was more adventurous and experimental and took risks and doing all that sort of thing,

00:21:55.000 --> 00:22:00.000
then I would really be ashamed of myself. And I think we did do those things.

00:22:00.000 --> 00:22:06.000
I mean, we introduced all kinds of new kinds of program genres

00:22:06.000 --> 00:22:16.000
and were able to go for audiences of a size in proportionate terms, which is much which you couldn't possibly do now.

00:22:16.000 --> 00:22:19.000
It's interesting. You say that it's key, then, which it is that there were three channels.

00:22:19.000 --> 00:22:27.000
Now that there are hundreds and may eventually be thousands, is it impossible to create the kind of television that was created then?

00:22:27.000 --> 00:22:35.000
Well, it only is impossible if you set yourself the wrong targets or say you're working within the wrong parameters.

00:22:35.000 --> 00:22:46.000
And the BBC is continually on a pendulum, you know, at one end, it's saying, oh, you're doing all these highbrow things and unpopular things.

00:22:46.000 --> 00:22:55.000
And you're up in the ivory tower. A lot of people don't resent paying the license holder because they don't see those kind of programs.

00:22:55.000 --> 00:22:59.000
Therefore, loosen up and get more popular. And then so you do.

00:22:59.000 --> 00:23:09.000
And then the poor BBC, having done that and proves itself to be more popular than commercial television, people say, oh, what a terrible thing that you're doing your public service obligations.

00:23:09.000 --> 00:23:16.000
And so it's continually doing that. And it's battered over there, whichever way it does.

00:23:16.000 --> 00:23:28.000
And it was certainly the case five years ago that everybody, politicians and people running the BBC and all kinds of other people were saying, you know, the BBC has got to be more popular.

00:23:28.000 --> 00:23:32.000
You know, we won't be able to get the license renewed unless we are.

00:23:32.000 --> 00:23:36.000
So they did move that way and they won't. But now they're moving back again.

00:23:36.000 --> 00:23:41.000
We hear that phrase self-denied. It's crucial in this period when the BBC is seeking a new charter.

00:23:41.000 --> 00:23:45.000
Public service broadcasting. What in your view does that mean?

00:23:45.000 --> 00:23:49.000
Well, it certainly doesn't mean there's any such thing as a public service program.

00:23:49.000 --> 00:24:01.000
Public service broadcasting is using a broadcasting network not to make money, but to try and cover the widest possible spectrum of viewer interests.

00:24:01.000 --> 00:24:08.000
And your success as a public service broadcaster is measured to a very considerable degree by the width of that spectrum.

00:24:08.000 --> 00:24:12.000
The number of people who come to all kinds of different kinds of programs.

00:24:12.000 --> 00:24:23.000
So it's a schedule. It's a total schedule. And you don't call yourself a public service broadcaster just because you happen to do a program about Norman architecture and put it out at quarter past three in the morning.

00:24:23.000 --> 00:24:26.000
You know, that's not public service broadcasting.

00:24:26.000 --> 00:24:38.000
And also the news is a crucial thing, the public service broadcast news, which is stands up for its values, not simply political values.

00:24:38.000 --> 00:24:45.000
We've had a horrible example of that, but also popularity values.

00:24:45.000 --> 00:24:51.000
Your news ought to do things because you think it's important news.

00:24:51.000 --> 00:25:00.000
And even if it's unpalatable news, and even if the audience turns away because they don't want to hear that particular aspect of the news, that's neither here nor there.

00:25:00.000 --> 00:25:06.000
You've got to do it. You should make it as accessible as you can and as interesting as you can.

00:25:06.000 --> 00:25:09.000
But you've got to do it. That's what public service broadcasting is about.

00:25:09.000 --> 00:25:15.000
The horrible example of what can happen in political news, I assume, is a reference to the Andrew Gilligan affair.

00:25:15.000 --> 00:25:16.000
Of course, yeah.

00:25:16.000 --> 00:25:19.000
And what was your view of that?

00:25:19.000 --> 00:25:30.000
Well, I think it pointed up, very importantly, the bad condition we've got vis-a-vis the governors of the BBC and the governance of the BBC.

00:25:30.000 --> 00:25:40.000
There wasn't any question, in my view, that the correct thing that should have happened, that had the governors been properly divorced from the executive,

00:25:40.000 --> 00:25:49.000
that when the government, the prime minister or whoever else said, we're going to have an inquiry, the chairman of the BBC should say,

00:25:49.000 --> 00:25:53.000
thank you, you've no need to do that. That's what we're here for. That's our job.

00:25:53.000 --> 00:25:55.000
You mind your business and we'll mind ours.

00:25:55.000 --> 00:26:01.000
We will have an inquiry because we are public-spirited people and we are not part of the executive and we will get to the bottom of the question.

00:26:01.000 --> 00:26:09.000
Now, they didn't do that because for the last 20 years, the executive and the governance have been merged.

00:26:09.000 --> 00:26:18.000
Now, the dangers of that are now very, very apparent and people at the moment are making sure within the BBC that that is being,

00:26:18.000 --> 00:26:24.000
I mean, the present chairman and the governors are bringing them apart and a very good thing too.

00:26:24.000 --> 00:26:26.000
Are you confident that the licence fee will survive?

00:26:26.000 --> 00:26:30.000
No, nobody should be confident the licence fee should survive.

00:26:30.000 --> 00:26:36.000
The licence fee happens to be the most important element in broadcasting in this country, in my view,

00:26:36.000 --> 00:26:39.000
and it is so important that nobody should ever take it for granted.

00:26:39.000 --> 00:26:48.000
Everybody should be saying, yes, we understand why that is there and we understand that the quality of broadcasting that there is in this country

00:26:48.000 --> 00:26:57.000
is actually a direct consequence of having one major organisation which broadcasts with a licence.

00:26:57.000 --> 00:27:01.000
So once you have hundreds of channels as we have now, we have the internet, we have everything else,

00:27:01.000 --> 00:27:08.000
why should British people, if they want to watch the BBC, still be required to pay a licence fee and go to jail if they don't?

00:27:08.000 --> 00:27:15.000
Why should you or I pay for our rates for a public library even though we never used it in our lives?

00:27:15.000 --> 00:27:19.000
Why should you or I pay for swimming pools for people if we don't?

00:27:19.000 --> 00:27:27.000
The notion of having a society with a sufficiently integrated and a sufficiently sense of social responsibility

00:27:27.000 --> 00:27:34.000
to pay commonly to make sure that everybody in the society has the right sort of facilities that they need,

00:27:34.000 --> 00:27:39.000
that's perfectly clear and that applies to broadcasting just as it does to public libraries.

00:27:39.000 --> 00:27:48.000
The sense, which when I read and talk to you about the early days in TV, that sense of wonder and hope, that can never exist again in TV.

00:27:48.000 --> 00:27:58.000
No, I mean I do remember very clearly thinking that this was wonderful because, and it was a monopoly situation,

00:27:58.000 --> 00:28:06.000
because the entire nation, bank clerks in Serberton could understand what coal miners in Durham were talking about

00:28:06.000 --> 00:28:13.000
and musicians could hear what writers were talking about and that the nation would come together

00:28:13.000 --> 00:28:21.000
and that we would all have a social cohesive and be that much better as a community as a consequence.

00:28:21.000 --> 00:28:32.000
And then commercialism came in and that distorted that, that ideal couldn't survive that.

00:28:32.000 --> 00:28:36.000
So David Attenborough, thank you.

00:29:02.000 --> 00:29:05.000
Thank you.
